{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791307000000-reviewer-d13-candidate-gate.opus.stdout.log /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791307000000-architect-d13-signoff-astra.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-06 16:49:42 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-06 23:49:42 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (opus-5-5, gate review)\n\n# Gate review: M-next D13, the driver's candidate half\n\n**Verdict: NOT READY.** The overall shape is right. There is one plain-data step state, the three remote paths are composition-supplied, the variant is checked against the fold's admitted set before lower, tenure is refolded every active step, and the release is cleanup only and gets retried. Three changes are required before landing:\n\n- **(F1)** Accept only grants that answer a proposal this driver sent.\n- **(F2)** Stop running `checkpoint/inspect` ahead of the D7 pipeline, because it reverses the 7.2.1 refusal order.\n- **(F3)** Stop the stale-grant branch from minting and sending a new proposal on every step.\n\n**(F4)** should also land in this slice: the driver's admit-routing code has no test, and I don't think that gap needs to wait for D14.\n\nScope: I read the engineer's report, the r3 plan (sections 1.4 to 1.7, 1.10, 1.11 and the D13 contract), the D10 lower-inputs ruling, the two new files, and the relevant parts of `dao.lease`, `holder/evidence`, `ledger` and `handoff`. Read-only: no edits, no suites run. The worktree is on `ucf-d13-candidate` at b38786c8, which contains 4c4764f7. The only uncommitted non-collab files are the two new ones.\n\n## Required\n\n**F1. The driver accepts grants it never proposed for.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:326-340, 551-558, 669-670`\n\n- **What happens:** `grants-to` keeps every `:accepted` fact naming `me` on the occurrence, and `step-proposing` accepts `(peek grants)`. Nothing checks the grant's `:dao.lease/proposal` against an id this driver minted.\n  - `lease/initial-holder` is built without the `:subject` and `:proposal` expectations. `dao.lease` provides those for exactly this case (`dao/lease.cljc:1583-1589`: \"a holder that proposed … will not keep-first an unsolicited or stale grant\").\n  - It even accepts on the very first `:proposing` step, before any proposal has been sent.\n- **Concrete failure:** a holder process crashes while its lease is still live, and the composition restarts a fresh driver for the same identity.\n  - The new driver finds the pre-crash grant, refolds, sees it is still the occurrence's live lease, and lowers and runs.\n  - `observe-grant` seeds the basis at the restart reading (`dao/lease.cljc:1788`). The holder's bound is therefore up to one full duration later than the judge's window (renewal at t0, restart near t0+D, believed bound near t0+2D).\n  - That removes the H3 guarantee that a holder stops before the judge's window closes. From then on, the only thing stopping this holder is the per-step ledger refold, which is a race against the judge's lapse record and the reader's lag. The regranted holder can start while this one still does at-least-once IO.\n  - This also contradicts r3 1.10 and D14's row \"a restarted holder with a journaled grant does not run; it releases and re-proposes\". D13's acceptance rule should not need D14 to undo it.\n- **Required change:**\n  - Keep the set of proposal ids this driver has sent, and accept only a grant whose `:dao.lease/proposal` is in that set.\n  - Pass `:subject` and `:proposal` into `initial-holder`.\n  - Treat any other grant to `me` as foreign. Whether to release it can wait for D14's ruling; at minimum, never activate on it.\n- **Test impact:** every test that uses `grant-direct!` with a pid the driver never minted (\"p-x\", \"p-a\", \"p-b\"; test lines 657, 881, 953 and others) must drive real proposals through the judge or use the driver's own pid. That is better evidence anyway (see F5).\n\n**F2. The candidate runs `checkpoint/inspect` before the D7 reader, so the refusal order is wrong.** `driver.cljc:894-904`\n\n- `step-validating` calls `checkpoint/inspect` first, and inspect checks the address first (`checkpoint.cljc:406-412`). Only after that does it call `handoff/resume-task`, whose D7 pipeline runs decode, the tag, the version gate and only then the address (`handoff.cljc:1821-1826`).\n- r3 1.7 says the resumer's order is the normative one for 7.2.1, and D7's contract pins \"unsupported version wins over a wrong address\".\n- Consequences in the driver:\n  - Bytes with version 2 under a wrong address answer `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` instead of `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  - Stream-codec version-0 bytes fail inside inspect's Jing decode and answer `:undecodable` instead of D7's answer.\n- **Fix:** call `resume-task` first. Then call `inspect` only on bytes D7 has accepted, purely to pull out the occurrence and arbitration. Alternatively, have the awaiting-grant answer carry them.\n- Add a driver test for \"wrong address plus unsupported version\" that expects `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. The existing junk-bytes test (test line 969) uses a correct address, so it cannot tell the two orders apart.\n\n**F3. A stale, unheld grant to `me` causes a new proposal on every step.** `driver.cljc:583-586`\n\n- **Mechanism:**\n  - When the newest grant to `me` is stale and the occurrence is unheld, `accept` calls `(propose (assoc state :proposal nil) projection)`.\n  - Clearing `:proposal` forces a mint, and `propose` sends it.\n  - On the next step `grants-to` returns the same stale grant, so the branch repeats: one new proposal id and one new append per step until something regrants.\n- This bypasses `propose`'s own \"resend unchanged while uncarried, mint only after a refusal\" rule (lines 475-478), which is the 7.7.8 discipline the report describes.\n- **Fix:** call `(propose state projection)` without clearing the proposal. Mint fresh only when the outstanding proposal is the one the stale grant answered. That check is easy once F1 tracks proposal ids.\n- Add a test: lapse the driver's own grant, step N times, and assert exactly one new proposal.\n\n**F4. The driver's admit routing has no test, and the D14 deferral is broader than it needs to be.** `driver.cljc:750-754`\n\n- The report is right that an enrolled write retained in a body is a successor and gets orphan-refused until D14.\n- A first-export body whose program issues an enrolled `:put` after activation carries no operation id, so it should not have that problem.\n  - Enrollment is available in this world: `authority/enroll!` is used by `writer_test.cljc:143` and line 263.\n  - So a test can lower a machine parked on a read of `prog-r`, feed it, and let it write to the enrolled target.\n- **What is untested:** the driver code that is new in D13:\n  - the `:yin.k/admit` and projected-`:yin.k/admission` dispatch in `drain`;\n  - the hand-off to `writer/discharge`;\n  - the machine continuing after discharge.\n- The at-least-once test (test line 905) asserts that no admit request occurs at all. `reader/settle` routing is covered by the regrant test, and the writer arm should get the same.\n- **Required:** one test of an enrolled write after activation through the driver, discharged by reply, plus one case discharged by a projected outcome.\n- If there is a reason a fresh enrolled put under a first-offer occurrence also needs a predecessor, the report should state it with the authority line that refuses it. Then the deferral stands as scoped.\n\n## Should fix (in this slice or recorded for D14)\n\n**F5. The residual-2 regrant rows bypass \"a candidate that proposes\".** Test lines 689-704, 785-793 and 846-854.\n\n- Each regrant is written straight into the ledger with `grant-direct!` and a foreign pid. In the post-grant-failure test the lease is also force-lapsed with `lapse-direct!` when the judge still holds it.\n- The D13 contract row is \"the occurrence is granted again to a candidate that proposes\". As written, the tests would pass even if the judge refused to regrant.\n- The renewal test does show the judge lapsing the released lease (line 850), which is good. Its follow-up should then let holder-b propose and the judge grant it. With F1 in place, that is forced anyway.\n\n**F6. If the ledger stays unreadable, the run never ends at the lease bound.** `driver.cljc:828-831`\n\n- An unreadable ledger answers `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before the clock is read.\n- No IO runs, so the \"IO stops\" half holds. But the run is never ended: the machine keeps gate `:running`, no diagnostic is appended and no release is sent, for as long as the ledger stays unreadable.\n- The bound check needs no ledger. Read the clock first, and if `holding?` is false, `end-run :lease-bound` regardless of what the ledger says.\n- Related, and fine as long as it's documented: one transient ledger error on a due step also skips that step's renewal.\n\n**F7. The current-tenure recheck compares the lease only, not the epoch.** `driver.cljc:834`\n\n- The D10 ruling and the report both describe the check as \"the fold's live lease at the binding's epoch\", but `step-active` compares only `l`.\n- `accept` does compare the epoch (line 550). Make the two consistent, or say in a comment that lease ids are never reused across epochs.\n\n**F8. The \"fold defect\" test does not reach a real fold defect.** Test line 721.\n\n- `(assoc rs 1 [arb {}])` gives a record with no `:t`. `history-defect` (line 295) reports that as `:fold-defect` before `ledger/fold-record` is ever called.\n- The path where `t` is dense and the content is defective (lines 542-546, then 574) is never exercised.\n- There is a real tension here. Some content defects are also invalid bindings: the report itself says a duplicated binding \"defects every successor's evidence fold\". The driver resolves that overlap toward a release. The report's claim \"never a release\" for unavailable history is therefore only true for defects detected by the t-check.\n- Add a case where `t` is dense but the content is defective and is not a binding. Assert unsatisfied with no release, and state the overlap rule in the docstring.\n\n**F9. The step state is not plain data.** `driver.cljc:185` and line 184, plus the holder at line 551.\n\n- `:seams` stores the composition's functions in the state.\n- `:receiver` is a live machine.\n- `:holder` contains `dao.lease`'s `:resolver` function, which the driver fabricates as `(fn [_ _] arb)`.\n- The authority boundary is still respected, because every remote touch goes through the seams. But the contract says \"one explicit plain-data step state\".\n- Prefer `(step env state)` with the seams in `env`, and keep only data in `state`. The fabricated resolver is defensible, since `grants-to` already filtered by the composition's attribution, but it deserves a comment.\n\n**F10. The driver duplicates D3's private history and fold logic.** `driver.cljc:275-312`, against `holder/evidence.cljc:36-72`.\n\n- `transport-error?`, `history-defect` and `fold` restate `evidence/defect` and `evidence/fold`.\n- Two copies of the complete-history-or-nothing rule can drift apart. Make D3's helpers public and call them.\n- `machines` and `ended` are a third copy of the walker that already exists in the writer and the reader. That pattern predates D13, but this would be the moment to collapse it.\n\n## Notes and assessments\n\n**N1. Contract rows, end to end**\n- **Row 1 (two candidates over two encodings):** covered, with one holder per encoding. Awaiting-grant, then not-holder naming the winner, nothing released, no further proposals while held.\n- **Row 2 (invalid bindings):** all four cases are covered. Each answers not-holder plus the release `[:yin.k/release \"lease-1\"]` with no machine. The extra \"another author's\" case also goes through `:no-binding`.\n- **Row 3 (unavailable history):** all four D3 modes; non-terminal `:proposing`, no machine, no release. See F8 for the gap in the fold-defect case.\n- **Row 4 (post-grant failure):** release, then a retained retry on a full inbound with an identical request id. Covered, apart from F5.\n- **Row 5 (renewal and bound):** renewal at elapsed 10 of 30, the basis advances to the pre-append reading, and exactly one value plus one diagnostic at the bound.\n  - Line 826, `(is (< 10 15))`, compares two literals and tests nothing. Replace it with a step at, say, elapsed 5 that asserts no renewal was sent.\n  - The bound assertion counts only inbound requests. Also assert that `@(:observations d)` did not change and that the program stream did not move during and after the bound step.\n- **Row 6 (source resumes only after its own grant):** covered, but via `grant-direct!` (F1 and F5).\n\n**N2. Admitted-variant proof.** Correct, and placed correctly.\n- Line 577 checks the address against `(:yin.k/variants entry)` from the driver's own fold of the attributed records. The order is binding, then evidence, then variant, then the stale checks, then holding, then lower.\n- The test at line 1000 proves the admitted set came from the ledger and not from comparing two supplied addresses.\n\n**N3. Tenure recheck.**\n- It runs before any execution or IO on every `:running` and `:safepoint` step.\n- It goes through a fresh ledger fold, not the stored `:ready` answer, and it calls `lease/holding?` on a new clock reading.\n- Order: ledger, liveness, clock and bound, then renewal, then the program cycle. That matches the D10 obligation, subject to F6 and F7.\n\n**N4. Release semantics.**\n- Cleanup only: the driver never sends completion evidence, never touches a quarantine, and becomes terminal `:failed` after the release, so it proposes nothing for recovery.\n- A pending release is retried verbatim (lines 880-887), and `lease/stop` is used correctly (line 432).\n- Two loose ends:\n  - `:release :carried` is set only by `control-reply`, which never runs in `:releasing`, so the `(true? (:carried release))` test at line 876 is dead.\n  - `:proposal :carried` is set at line 636 but never read.\n- `:stale` also releases a lease the fold already shows as gone. That is harmless, but r3 1.6 says to release only \"if the lease may still be live\".\n\n**N5. One `vm/run` per step.** I accept this as a scheduling choice.\n- A tenure check before every execution slice is the conservative granularity, and it matches D12's \"the driver steps the machine and calls again\".\n- The real cost is elsewhere: every step refolds the whole ledger from the origin. Replaying a prefix of N inputs therefore costs O(N × history). r3's risk 3 budgeted O(history) per grant, not per step.\n- `ledger/fold-record` is incremental, so the state could carry the projection and the last `t`. That's not blocking on the memory substrate, but D15 should record it.\n\n**N6. The engineer's stated limits.**\n- **Enrolled writer arm deferred to D14:** too broad; see F4.\n- **Judge release-lapse only exercised where the judge granted:** that is accurate, and it's a symptom of the `grant-direct!` setup (F5).\n- **\"Two clock readings per accepting step\":** the report is wrong here, in the code's favour. `accept` reads the clock once (line 557) and uses that reading for both `observe-grant` and the lower's `:now` (line 605). `step-active` also reads it once (line 841). The report should be corrected.\n- **`:safepoint` and the exit half belonging to D14:** correctly scoped.\n\n**N7. Portability and style.**\n- `record-facts` (line 321) uses `for` over `(distinct (map first datoms))`. On ClojureDart, `for` over a chunked seq longer than 32 can hand the body a nil and crash, Dart only. A transaction with more than 32 entities would hit it. Use `mapv` or `keep`.\n- `lower`'s `base` takes the first entry of a single-entry reading map; that is fine on all three hosts.\n- The tenure `:bound` passed to D10 ignores a `:dao.lease/max` cap. D10's `n < b` check is looser than `holding?`, which does apply the cap. Harmless, but worth a comment.\n- No kondo or cljstyle run was available to me; I rely on the orchestrator's 0/1-pre-existing and clean results.\n- Only JVM was run. The Node and Dart lanes belong to the landing gate.\n- The branch is behind master by the Python C3 and yin.repl commits. They don't touch these files, but it needs a rebase before landing.\n\n## What must change, in order\n\n1. Accept only grants that answer this driver's own proposal ids, with `:subject` and `:proposal` passed to the dao.lease holder, and rework the tests off foreign-pid `grant-direct!` (F1, F5).\n2. Make the D7 reader run first in `:validating`, and add the profile-before-address test (F2).\n3. Stop minting a proposal on every step in the stale-and-unheld branch, and add a one-proposal test (F3).\n4. Add the enrolled-write-after-activation test for the admit routing, by reply and by projected outcome, or put the authority's reason for refusing it in the report (F4).\n\nF6 to F10 and N1's test tightening should go in with these if they are cheap. Otherwise record them in the report for D14 or D15.\nThe driver currently treats acceptance into the front’s request stream as successful renewal or release. The front has a separate authenticated `:carried` response, so I’m checking that distinction and whether tenure is rechecked after guest execution, before program IO.\n\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-06 16:48:34 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-06 23:48:34 +0700\n\nCHANGES\n\n1. **Renewal advances tenure before carriage is proved.** In `driver.cljc:718–732`, `renew` passes the front-inbound append result to `lease/observe-renewal`. That only proves the request was queued: `authority/front.cljc:211–226` can subsequently answer `:suspended` without carrying the renewal. Retain the request and its pre-send clock reading; advance tenure only on authenticated, matching `:carried` evidence. Recheck the existing bound before accepting delayed evidence; a late acknowledgment must never revive expired tenure. Pin inbound acceptance followed by failed carriage, delayed acknowledgment, and successful carriage.\n\n2. **Proposal and release progress can be lost.** `control-reply` (`driver.cljc:630–643`) marks carriage without checking `:yin.k/answer`; `step-releasing` (`873–887`) declares release complete on inbound acceptance and stops reading replies. Proposals likewise stop resending once queued. Retain these obligations until authenticated carriage, retry unchanged requests after suspended carriage or lost replies, and distinguish lease-proposal rejection from transport/carriage failure. Pin a full lease-fact stream behind an accepting inbound stream: proposals must eventually progress and releases must eventually be carried, without clearing quarantine or manufacturing completion.\n\n3. **The tenure check does not cover subsequent IO.** `step-active` checks once (`driver.cljc:840–847`), then `run-cycle` executes `vm/run`, writer emission and live observation (`778–788`) without another check. Computation or an earlier effect can consume the remaining tenure. Enforce the D10 ruling’s recheck before subsequent program IO, including bare writes and closes; checking only the request callback misses those direct effects. Pin expiry during computation and between successive effects: no further program IO, root and children ended, release retained for cleanup.\n\n4. **Thrown post-grant failures bypass cleanup.** `run-cycle` and `step-active` have no failure transition around execution/emission. The landed writer explicitly throws for terminal bare-link append outcomes (`holder/writer.cljc:278`), so this is a reachable path, not hypothetical seam misuse. Preserve the failure while transitioning into an ended state with a retained release obligation; do not retry the failed program effect. Pin that terminal-link path and a release initially unable to be carried.\n\nThe admitted-variant proof and composition of the existing lower, writer and reader are appropriate, but these defects violate the remote-carriage, tenure and post-grant cleanup contracts. The current tests carry the front immediately after driver steps, which masks the distinction between inbound acceptance and actual carriage. These are D13 obligations, not D14 journal work. No files were edited and no suites were run.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md; git status --short; rg --files | rg 'candidate|d13.*findings|AGENTS.md|holder.*driver|driver.*test'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md; git status --short; rg --files | rg 'candidate|d13.*findings|AGENTS.md|holder.*driver|driver.*test'\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n?? collab/1791192700000-architect-m-next-d-plan-r2-confirm.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n?? collab/1791194000000-architect-m-next-d-plan-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n?? collab/1791203000000-architect-d6-link-cursor-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n?? collab/1791240000000-architect-d10-lower-inputs-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n?? collab/1791299000000-compiler-engineer-ucf-d13-candidate.findings.md\n?? collab/1791299000000-compiler-engineer-ucf-d13-candidate.prompt.md\n?? src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\n?? test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc\nAGENTS.md\ncollab/1791299000000-compiler-engineer-ucf-d13-candidate.findings.md\ncollab/1791299000000-compiler-engineer-ucf-d13-candidate.prompt.md\ntest/dao/gui/event/fixtures/arena_owned-candidate-omitted.edn\ntest/dao/stream/waitset/driver_test.cljs\ntest/dao/stream/waitset/driver_test.clj\ntest/dao/stream/waitset/driver_test.cljd\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc\ntest/yin/repl/driver_test.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
