{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -12 /private/tmp/d13-focused-final.log; tail -8 /private/tmp/d13-kondo-final.log; rg -n 'F9|F10' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791307000000-reviewer-d13-candidate-gate.opus.stdout.log -A12; sed -n '110,135p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1791307000000-reviewer-d13-candidate-gate.opus.stdout.log; sed -n '1580,1640p' src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"Testing yin.vm.ucf.holder.evidence-test\n\nTesting yin.vm.ucf.holder.export-test\n\nTesting yin.vm.ucf.holder.reader-test\n\nTesting yin.vm.ucf.holder.writer-test\n\nTesting yin.vm.ucf.ledger-test\n\nRan 178 tests containing 1705 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\nLoading initial Timbre config from: :default\nlinting took 2211ms, errors: 0, warnings: 0\n96:**F9. The step state is not plain data.** `driver.cljc:185` and line 184, plus the holder at line 551.\n97-\n98-- `:seams` stores the composition's functions in the state.\n99-- `:receiver` is a live machine.\n100-- `:holder` contains `dao.lease`'s `:resolver` function, which the driver fabricates as `(fn [_ _] arb)`.\n101-- The authority boundary is still respected, because every remote touch goes through the seams. But the contract says \"one explicit plain-data step state\".\n102-- Prefer `(step env state)` with the seams in `env`, and keep only data in `state`. The fabricated resolver is defensible, since `grants-to` already filtered by the composition's attribution, but it deserves a comment.\n103-\n104:**F10. The driver duplicates D3's private history and fold logic.** `driver.cljc:275-312`, against `holder/evidence.cljc:36-72`.\n105-\n106-- `transport-error?`, `history-defect` and `fold` restate `evidence/defect` and `evidence/fold`.\n107-- Two copies of the complete-history-or-nothing rule can drift apart. Make D3's helpers public and call them.\n108-- `machines` and `ended` are a third copy of the walker that already exists in the writer and the reader. That pattern predates D13, but this would be the moment to collapse it.\n109-\n110-## Notes and assessments\n111-\n112-**N1. Contract rows, end to end**\n113-- **Row 1 (two candidates over two encodings):** covered, with one holder per encoding. Awaiting-grant, then not-holder naming the winner, nothing released, no further proposals while held.\n114-- **Row 2 (invalid bindings):** all four cases are covered. Each answers not-holder plus the release `[:yin.k/release \"lease-1\"]` with no machine. The extra \"another author's\" case also goes through `:no-binding`.\n115-- **Row 3 (unavailable history):** all four D3 modes; non-terminal `:proposing`, no machine, no release. See F8 for the gap in the fold-defect case.\n116-- **Row 4 (post-grant failure):** release, then a retained retry on a full inbound with an identical request id. Covered, apart from F5.\n--\n165:F6 to F10 and N1's test tightening should go in with these if they are cheap. Otherwise record them in the report for D14 or D15.\n## Notes and assessments\n\n**N1. Contract rows, end to end**\n- **Row 1 (two candidates over two encodings):** covered, with one holder per encoding. Awaiting-grant, then not-holder naming the winner, nothing released, no further proposals while held.\n- **Row 2 (invalid bindings):** all four cases are covered. Each answers not-holder plus the release `[:yin.k/release \"lease-1\"]` with no machine. The extra \"another author's\" case also goes through `:no-binding`.\n- **Row 3 (unavailable history):** all four D3 modes; non-terminal `:proposing`, no machine, no release. See F8 for the gap in the fold-defect case.\n- **Row 4 (post-grant failure):** release, then a retained retry on a full inbound with an identical request id. Covered, apart from F5.\n- **Row 5 (renewal and bound):** renewal at elapsed 10 of 30, the basis advances to the pre-append reading, and exactly one value plus one diagnostic at the bound.\n  - Line 826, `(is (< 10 15))`, compares two literals and tests nothing. Replace it with a step at, say, elapsed 5 that asserts no renewal was sent.\n  - The bound assertion counts only inbound requests. Also assert that `@(:observations d)` did not change and that the program stream did not move during and after the bound step.\n- **Row 6 (source resumes only after its own grant):** covered, but via `grant-direct!` (F1 and F5).\n\n**N2. Admitted-variant proof.** Correct, and placed correctly.\n- Line 577 checks the address against `(:yin.k/variants entry)` from the driver's own fold of the attributed records. The order is binding, then evidence, then variant, then the stale checks, then holding, then lower.\n- The test at line 1000 proves the admitted set came from the ledger and not from comparing two supplied addresses.\n\n**N3. Tenure recheck.**\n- It runs before any execution or IO on every `:running` and `:safepoint` step.\n- It goes through a fresh ledger fold, not the stored `:ready` answer, and it calls `lease/holding?` on a new clock reading.\n- Order: ledger, liveness, clock and bound, then renewal, then the program cycle. That matches the D10 obligation, subject to F6 and F7.\n\n**N4. Release semantics.**\n- Cleanup only: the driver never sends completion evidence, never touches a quarantine, and becomes terminal `:failed` after the release, so it proposes nothing for recovery.\n- A pending release is retried verbatim (lines 880-887), and `lease/stop` is used correctly (line 432).\n- Two loose ends:\n  - `:release :carried` is set only by `control-reply`, which never runs in `:releasing`, so the `(true? (:carried release))` test at line 876 is dead.\n                     the per-unit quot bound, run after `check-units!` --\n                     so no first `due-to-renew?` can throw on it (assembly\n                     parity with the judge's N4/R3 tolerance gate)\n  :subject           optional expectation: when present, only a grant for\n                     this subject is held -- a holder that proposed for one\n                     resource will not keep-first an unsolicited or stale\n                     grant naming it for another\n  :proposal          optional expectation: when present, only a grant\n                     answering this proposal identity is held; a grant\n                     carrying no proposal does not match it\n\n  Missing or mis-shaped seams throw here, at assembly: a holder with no\n  working attribution would observe no grant, hold nothing, and falsely\n  never renew -- the same failure `initial-judge` refuses at assembly.\"\n  [{:keys [self grantor resolver renewal-interval] :as config}]\n  (let [units (get config :units default-units)]\n    (check-assembly! (some? self)\n                     \"the holder needs its identity: the :dao.lease/holder it observes grants for\"\n                     {:self self})\n    (check-assembly! (some? grantor)\n                     \"the holder needs the author its attribution returns for the grantor\"\n                     {:grantor grantor})\n    (check-assembly! (fn? resolver)\n                     \"the holder needs an attribution resolver: (fn [source fact] -> author)\"\n                     {:resolver resolver})\n    (check-assembly! (duration? renewal-interval)\n                     \"the renewal interval is a single-entry {unit positive-integer} map\"\n                     {:renewal-interval renewal-interval :refused :renewal-interval})\n    (check-units! units)\n    ;; The interval gets the tolerance's checks, run after check-units!:\n    ;; unit membership, then the per-unit bound -- taken by division, so\n    ;; the check itself cannot overflow and no later arithmetic on the\n    ;; interval can either (N4/R3 parity with the judge's tolerance).\n    (let [unit (first (keys renewal-interval))\n          magnitude (get renewal-interval unit)]\n      (check-assembly! (some? (get units unit))\n                       \"the renewal interval's unit is in the unit table\"\n                       {:renewal-interval renewal-interval :units units\n                        :refused :renewal-interval})\n      (check-assembly! (<= magnitude (quot magnitude-limit (get units unit)))\n                       \"the renewal interval's magnitude is within the per-unit bound\"\n                       {:renewal-interval renewal-interval :units units\n                        :refused :renewal-interval}))\n    {:self self\n     :grantor grantor\n     :resolver resolver\n     :units units\n     :renewal-interval renewal-interval\n     :subject (get config :subject)\n     :proposal (get config :proposal)\n     :grant nil\n     :granted-at nil\n     :last-renewal-at nil\n     :released? false\n     :bound-reached? false\n     :undersized? false}))\n\n\n(defn renewal-interval\n  \"The composition's renewal interval, validated against the duration it\n  is sized to (H2 as amended): strictly below HALF the duration -- equality\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
